<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE topic SYSTEM "https://resources.jetbrains.com/writerside/1.0/xhtml-entities.dtd">
<topic xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
       xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation="https://resources.jetbrains.com/writerside/1.0/topic.v2.xsd"
       id="determinism" title="Determinism">

    <show-structure for="chapter,procedure" depth="2"/>

    <p>
        The guarantee holds under one plan, one weaver version and one detail level. Weave a
        class file under all three unchanged, and the bytes come back identical. Your machine
        and the build server agree. Nothing woven records where it was built, or when.
    </p>

    <code-block lang="mermaid">
flowchart TB
    T["The target class file"] --> R["Weaving"]
    W["Parsed weaves, put in one order"] --> P["The plan and its fingerprint"]
    G["Loaded plugins"] --> P
    P --> R
    V["Weaver version"] --> R
    R --> O["Woven bytes, stamped"]
    </code-block>

    <chapter title="What decides the bytes" id="inputs">
        <p>
            Each class is woven from its own bytes. The plan was fixed before the run began. The
            order classes arrive in changes nothing.
        </p>

        <deflist type="full">
            <def title="The target class file">
                <p>
                    <code>Weaver.weave</code> takes an internal name and bytes, and nothing else.
                    It never sees a path or a file date, so neither can reach the output.
                </p>
            </def>
            <def title="A dissolving weave's own class file">
                <p>
                    A dissolving weave contributes its own class file too. Its method bodies are
                    copied out of that file, not out of the parsed model. That file is an input
                    in the same sense the target's is.
                </p>
            </def>
            <def title="One order, settled before the first class">
                <p>
                    The planner sorts every declaration once. Conflict detection, the fingerprint
                    and the report then all read that one sequence.
                    <a href="ordering.topic">Ordering</a> has the four parts of the key it sorts
                    by.
                </p>
            </def>
            <def title="The fingerprint">
                <p>
                    A SHA-256 of what the plan will do, never of where its weaves were found.
                    <a href="pipeline.topic">The weaving pipeline</a>
            <a href="weave-discovery.topic">How a weave is found</a> lists what goes into it and
                    what the weaver does with it.
                </p>
            </def>
            <def title="A name the weaver has to invent">
                <p>
                    A <code>@Unique</code> member that collides takes a suffix from a digest of
                    the weave's own name, never from a counter. So it lands alike everywhere.
                    <a href="weave-members.topic">Weave members</a> has the form.
                </p>
            </def>
        </deflist>
    </chapter>

    <chapter title="What moves them" id="moves">
        <table style="header-row">
            <tr>
                <td>What changed</td>
                <td>The fingerprint</td>
                <td>The bytes</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>A weave's targets, selectors or members</td>
                <td>moves</td>
                <td>move</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>The directory a weave was found in</td>
                <td>holds</td>
                <td>hold</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>The order two dissolving weaves are <a href="weave-discovery.topic">discovered</a> in</td>
                <td>moves</td>
                <td>move</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>Which plugins load, or what they register</td>
                <td>moves</td>
                <td>move</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>The detail level of the annotation</td>
                <td>holds</td>
                <td>move</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>The weaver version</td>
                <td>holds</td>
                <td>move</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>The compiler that produced the target, or a dissolving weave</td>
                <td>holds</td>
                <td>move</td>
            </tr>
        </table>

        <p>
            A moved fingerprint is a different plan. At build time, a class some other plan
            already stamped is refused as <code>AW2201</code>. Build the module clean. If that is
            not it, the input has been woven before. Weave the original classes instead.
            <a href="choose-a-driver.topic">Choose a driver</a> has what a load-time driver does.
        </p>

        <warning>
            <p>
                Watch the three rows where the fingerprint holds and the bytes move. A class the
                current plan already stamped is skipped regardless. An upgraded weaver, a new
                compiler or a changed detail level reaches only what is rebuilt. Delete the
                output directory after any of them.
            </p>
        </warning>
    </chapter>

    <chapter title="What it does not cover" id="limits">
        <deflist type="full">
            <def title="What the woven code does">
                <p>
                    The bytes are fixed; the behaviour is your handler's. A handler that reads a
                    clock or iterates a hash set gives a different run every time, and nothing
                    here reports it.
                </p>
            </def>
            <def title="What a plugin contributes">
                <p>
                    Its metadata is folded into the fingerprint. Only the value reaches the
                    annotation unchanged. The key gains the plugin's namespace as a prefix, and
                    entries are written in key order, not the order the plugin gave them. Under
                    <code>Woven.Detail.NONE</code> no annotation is written at all, so no class
                    carries it. A plugin publishing a build time moves every fingerprint on every
                    run. <a href="plugins.topic">Extending the engine</a> covers what a plugin may
                    register.
                </p>
            </def>
            <def title="The compiler in front of it">
                <p>
                    A JDK upgrade can change the class file <code>javac</code> emits. That reaches
                    a dissolving weave's own file as well as the target's. The weave output
                    follows, and a committed fixture moves with nothing wrong.
                </p>
            </def>
            <def title="The JDK the weaver runs on">
                <p>
                    The written bytes come from the JDK's own class file writer, not from
                    anything this repository controls. That JDK is part of the toolchain this
                    guarantee is stated over.
                </p>
            </def>
        </deflist>
    </chapter>

    <chapter title="Proving it in a test" id="testkit">
        <p>
            The testkit weaves every target twice, over the original bytes both times. Both
            results are kept. The comparison costs your test one line.
        </p>

        <code-block lang="java">
assertThatWoven(weaving.weave(Ledger.class))
        .wasWoven()
        .isDeterministic();
        </code-block>

        <p>
            <code>isDeterministic()</code> compares the two passes byte for byte. A
            constant-pool difference that changes no behaviour fails it, which is the point.
            Only the bytes are compared. The second pass's diagnostics are dropped before the
            result is built. A warning only it raises is lost.
        </p>

        <p>
            Two more checks build on this one and compare against a committed fixture.
            <a href="testing-woven-code.topic">Testing a weave</a> covers both.
        </p>
    </chapter>

